{
  "image_name": "3afe7960c9cc7c2d8e84f9b80b97e815.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two ways of thinking about dissolved organic matter (DOM) in the ocean and links them to DOM age, abundance, and degradation controls. At the top, DOM is divided into pools spanning very short-lived to ultra-refractory material. The visual emphasizes that the vast majority of the inventory is old, refractory DOM, with much smaller amounts of more labile fractions. A time scale underneath runs from seconds to millennia, showing that older pools persist far longer.\n\nThe middle section compares two conceptual models for what remains after carbon is first assimilated. In the “emergent recalcitrance” view, DOM is continuously reworked by microbes across all timescales, producing a gradually diminishing leftover pool rather than a sharply distinct refractory end product. In the “intrinsic recalcitrance” view, DOM progresses from labile to transient to refractory forms, implying the formation of inherently resistant molecules, with some later abiotic removal.\n\nThe bottom section summarizes what limits DOM degradation in each view. Under emergent recalcitrance, degradation depends strongly on organismal traits and ecological interactions, along with DOM concentration and composition. Under intrinsic recalcitrance, the emphasis shifts more toward molecular structure and constituent concentration as key controls.",
